/S /Figure
/K [15]
/P 1820 0 R
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1822 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (d\) The unloading point for flammable chemicals must be located at a safe distance outside the storage dyke.)
/K [0]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1823 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ( )
/K [1]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1824 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (e\) Pressurizing with air or inert gas for unloading should be avoided. Instead, pumps or vacuum systems should be used. Pumps should preferably be seal-less, and valves should be glandless. Bulk solid chemicals should be handled using lifting machines and conveyors.)
/K [2]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1825 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (f\) The coupling used to connect the hose to the tanker must be leak-proof. Flange connections are preferred, and if threaded connections are used, they must be protected against corrosion and wear. Thorough inspection should be conducted before making any connections.)
/K [3]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1826 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (g\) The unloading hose must be free from cracks and blisters and capable of withstanding the pressure developed during unloading. It should be hydro-tested at appropriate intervals based on experience, with proper records maintained.)
/K [4]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1827 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (h\) The same hose must not be used for unloading different chemicals. Hoses designated for different chemicals should be marked with distinct color stripes for easy identification.)
/K [5]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1828 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (i\) For flammable chemicals, both the tanker and the hose must be properly earthed and bonded before commencing the unloading operation.)
/K [6]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1829 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (j\) The unloading process must be carried out under the direct supervision of responsible staff authorized by the management.)
/K [7]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1830 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (k\) Operating personnel must wear appropriate personal protective clothing and equipment. Suitable breathing canisters and a first aid kit must be readily available at the site in case of an emergency.)
/K [8]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1831 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (l\) An ample supply of water or a suitable neutralizing medium must be available to manage any leaks or spills. Additionally, steam and inert gas hose stations must be provided at the unloading point.)
/K [9]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1832 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (m\) The unloading site must be well-illuminated, with flame-proof fittings used wherever necessary.)
/K [10]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1833 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (n\) The unloading system must include a facility to vent or drain any residual chemicals in the hose to a safe disposal point. When not in use, the hose should be properly blinded. A thermal safety valve discharging to a safe disposal or handling facility should also be installed.)
/K [11]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1834 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (o\) Before initiating unloading, the ullage of the receiving tank must be checked to prevent overflow. Gas or chemical leak detection systems should be installed to trigger an alarm at the control room or site, allowing for quick remedial action. Wherever necessary, quick-acting or remote isolation valves should be installed.)
/K [12]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1835 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (p\) Fire alarms and firefighting facilities, as specified in the data sheet, must be available at the unloading point and must align with the nature of the chemical being handled.)
/K [13]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
1836 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (q\) An effective communication system, such as a public address system, telephone, or other means, must be available for immediate contact with the control room, fire station, and health unit.)
/K [14]
/Pg 1251 0 R
>>
endobj
